{"name":"White Acorn","hex":"#d7a98c","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"},{"term":"acorn","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The fruit of the oak, being an oval nut growing in a woody cup or cupule.","A cone-shaped piece of wood on the point of the spindle above the vane, on the mast-head."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/acorn"}],"entries":[{"title":"Acorn","blocks":[{"text":"The acorn is the nut of the oaks and their close relatives ; alternatively known as glans. It usually contains a seedling surrounded by two cotyledons, enclosed in a tough shell known as the pericarp, and borne in a cup-shaped cupule. Acorns take between 5 and 24 months to mature; see the list of Quercus species for details of oak classification, in which acorn morphology and phenology are important factors."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Quercus_robur_acorn_-_Keila.jpg/330px-Quercus_robur_acorn_-_Keila.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acorn","wikidata":"Q3914781"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Acorns_small_to_large.jpg/500px-Acorns_small_to_large.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Acorns_small_to_large.jpg/1280px-Acorns_small_to_large.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Acorns_small_to_large.jpg/500px-Acorns_small_to_large.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Acorns of the willow oak in South Carolina (from small to large, counterclockwise from center): Q. phellos (willow oak), Q. falcata (southern red oak; top right), Q. alba (white oak), and Q. coccinea (scarlet oak). Scale bar at upper right is 1 cm.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAcorns_small_to_large.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Ponies_eating_acorns_on_Parkhill_Lawn%2C_New_Forest_-_geograph.org.uk_-_251840.jpg/330px-Ponies_eating_acorns_on_Parkhill_Lawn%2C_New_Forest_-_geograph.org.uk_-_251840.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Ponies_eating_acorns_on_Parkhill_Lawn%2C_New_Forest_-_geograph.org.uk_-_251840.jpg/330px-Ponies_eating_acorns_on_Parkhill_Lawn%2C_New_Forest_-_geograph.org.uk_-_251840.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Horses eating acorns.  Acorns can cause painful death in equines, especially if eaten in excess.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wik